Z||000000||gixxer||06-19-2000||08:06 PM||||There is a clamp mounted at the very TOP of the steering rack, just to the right (more toward the center) of the pinion adjust screw. The lower part of the clamp is moulded into the body of the rack. The upper half is removable with one bolt. The clamped part, if it had one, would be oriented in line with the rack body.<BR>The inner diameter of the clamp is about 32mm. The car is a Formula, manufactured 3/88. Lock to lock takes 3 1/4 turns. There is nothing else visible on the rack that would indicate that this is part of a steering damper system. I haven't checked under the boots yet. <BR>Thanx||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||06-20-2000||01:19 AM||||Could it be a provision for power steering?  I read somewhere that 1988 Fieros were designed to accept a power steering unit (electrically powered? -can't remember for sure).  According to the story, you could order the power steering unit from GM dealers, even though no Fieros were delivered from the factory with power steering.  <P>Don't know if the story is true or not...<P>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-20-2000||09:27 AM||||The proposed p/steering unit was an electro-hydraulic unit.  The factory only put those on the cars with the factory raspberry paint and the Lotus suspension.  Sorry.<P>gixxer--If you can turn the wheel 3 1/4 turns, maybe something has broken.  As far as I know, there were only 2 different racks.  The "Sport" rack was 3 turns lock-to-lock and the other was 3 1/2 turns.||||reg||1
